## State Management

- **Use Zustand** for global client state management. The template ships **no** store by default; add stores inside FSD slices (`entities/<name>/model/` for domain state) — there is no root `store/` directory.
- **Each store should be in its own folder** with tests alongside (e.g., `entities/user/model/userStore.ts` + `userStore.test.ts`)
- **Use the `createSelectors` utility** from `shared/lib/utils-store/createSelectors.ts` for auto-selectors
- **Store exports both regular selector and auto-selector**: `useStore((state) => state.value)` or `useStore.use.value()`
- **Server state**: prefer Server Components / Route Handlers (default). **TanStack Query is opt-in** — it is not in the default `package.json`; add it in-repo only when a client-side cache is needed.
- **Keep local state in components** when possible, use Zustand for shared state
- **Client Components only** - Zustand stores must be used in Client Components ('use client')

## Store Structure

- **Modular store pattern**: Each domain has its own store folder
- **Tests next to store files** (`storeName.test.ts`)
- **Direct imports** (no `index.ts` re-exports) for optimal tree-shaking
- **Store-related files** (types, utils) can be added to the same folder when needed

### Example Structure

```
entities/
  ├── user/
  │   └── model/
  │       ├── userStore.ts
  │       └── userStore.test.ts
  └── settings/
      └── model/
          ├── settingsStore.ts
          └── settingsStore.test.ts

shared/lib/utils-store/
  └── createSelectors.ts        # shared auto-selector helper
```

### Example Usage

```typescript
// entities/user/model/userStore.ts
'use client';

import { create } from 'zustand';
import { createSelectors } from '@/shared/lib/utils-store/createSelectors';

const useUserStoreBase = create(...);
export const useUserStore = createSelectors(useUserStoreBase);

// Import directly
import { useUserStore } from '@/entities/user/model/userStore';

// In Client Component
'use client';

export const UserProfile = () => {
    const username = useUserStore.use.username();
    // or
    const username = useUserStore((state) => state.username);
    return <div>{username}</div>;
};
```

## TanStack Query in Next.js (opt-in — not in the default template)

The default `package.json` has no `@tanstack/react-query`, and `app/providers.tsx`
wires analytics only. Add the dependency and a `QueryClientProvider` first if a fork
needs the patterns below.

- **Provider in `app/providers.tsx`** - wrap the app with `QueryClientProvider`
- **Server Components**: Use `fetch` directly or Server Actions
- **Client Components**: Use `useQuery`, `useMutation` hooks
- **Server-side data fetching**: Use async Server Components

### Server Component Data Fetching

```typescript
// app/blog/[id]/page.tsx - Server Component
export default async function BlogPost({ params }: Props) {
    const { id } = await params;
    const post = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/posts/${id}`).then(r => r.json());
    return <article>{post.content}</article>;
}
```

### Client Component Data Fetching

```typescript
// components/PostList.tsx - Client Component
'use client';

import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';

export const PostList = () => {
    const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({
        queryKey: ['posts'],
        queryFn: () => fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json())
    });

    if (isLoading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
    return <div>{data.map(post => <div key={post.id}>{post.title}</div>)}</div>;
};
```
